/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · AI & the Future (batch z) */
WB.L("mechanical-turk", "ai", "beginner", "The Mechanical Turk: a chess machine with a secret",
  "For over 80 years, a turbaned automaton beat chess players across Europe and America. The truth inside its cabinet still echoes through modern AI.",
  [
    ["A chess-playing marvel", "In 1770 Wolfgang von Kempelen unveiled a chess-playing 'machine', and its demonstrations were staged to convince people nothing was hidden.",
      "In 1770 the engineer Wolfgang von Kempelen presented a chess-playing 'machine' to Empress Maria Theresa's court in Vienna. A life-sized figure in Ottoman-style robes and a turban sat behind a large wooden cabinet with a chessboard on top. Its mechanical arm grasped pieces and moved them, and it shook its head at illegal moves. It usually won. Kempelen seemed slightly embarrassed by the fame and reportedly kept it packed away for years.\n\nBefore each game, the operator opened the cabinet doors one at a time, shining a candle through a tangle of cogs and levers, and lifted the robes to show more machinery. The whirring was mostly for effect. Never seeing everything at once, onlookers felt sure nothing was hidden."],
    ["Who was inside", "A strong human chess player hid inside the cabinet, using a sliding seat, magnets and levers to play.",
      "The secret was simple and daring: a skilled chess player was hidden inside. A moving seat let him slide along as each door opened, keeping him behind panels and fake machinery. Each piece on the board carried a magnet that tugged a small metal marker, so he could follow the game. He planned moves on his own small pegboard and steered the Turk's arm with levers. A candle gave light, and smoke escaped through the figure's turban.\n\nOver the decades, a series of strong players worked as operators, cramped and sweating in the dark. The theatre worked: people left certain the machine held only machinery, exactly as intended."],
    ["Fame, doubt and fire", "The Turk toured for decades, sceptics such as Edgar Allan Poe argued a human was hidden inside, and a fire destroyed it in 1854.",
      "In the 1780s the Turk toured Europe, and accounts say it played Benjamin Franklin in Paris. After Kempelen died in 1804, the showman Johann Nepomuk Maelzel, who also promoted the metronome, bought it. Stories say it played Napoleon in 1809, though some tales were embroidered, so hold them loosely. Maelzel took it to the United States in 1826, drawing large paying crowds.\n\nNot everyone was fooled. In 1836 Edgar Allan Poe argued in an essay that a person must be concealed inside, noting that a true machine would win every game while the Turk sometimes lost. Some of his other reasoning was wrong. The Turk burned in a Philadelphia museum in 1854, and only then did a son of its last owner publish the secret."],
    ["Artificial artificial intelligence", "Amazon Mechanical Turk, launched in 2005, openly pays people to do tasks that computers find hard.",
      "The Turk's name came back in 2005, when Amazon launched Amazon Mechanical Turk. It lets businesses post small online tasks that are easy for people but hard for computers, such as describing a photo, checking whether two products are the same or transcribing a blurry receipt. Thousands of workers around the world complete them for small payments.\n\nAmazon called the idea 'artificial artificial intelligence'. Like the original Turk, it looks like a computer doing the work, but humans are quietly inside. Platforms like this became a major source of the labelled data that later trained machine learning systems, including image recognisers."],
    ["Humans behind the curtain", "Much of today's AI still depends on hidden human labour, so it pays to ask who or what is really doing the work.",
      "The Turk's lesson is still relevant. Modern AI systems learn from huge amounts of data that people have created, sorted and labelled, often low-paid workers in many countries. Humans also rate chatbot answers to teach them which responses are helpful, and moderate the disturbing content that filters miss. Some start-ups have been caught presenting services as automated while staff did much of the work behind the scenes.\n\nNone of this means AI is fake. It means the line between machine and human effort is often blurrier than it looks. Like eighteenth century audiences, we tend to see intelligence where clever presentation invites us to."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the operator open the cabinet doors one at a time before each game?", ["To let the hidden player stretch his legs","To cool the clockwork before play began","To convince onlookers the cabinet held only machinery","To let the opponent inspect the chess pieces"], 2, "The ritual was theatre: showing the inside piece by piece made people believe they had seen everything."],
    [1, "How did the hidden player follow the game on the board?", ["Each piece carried a magnet that tugged a marker he could see","He watched through a small mirror in the turban","An assistant called out every move","He listened to the clicking of the gears"], 0, "Magnets and levers let a human follow and steer the game while staying out of sight."],
    [2, "What was Poe's sharp point about the Turk?", ["Its arm moved too smoothly for clockwork","A true machine would win every game, yet it sometimes lost","It never made illegal moves","Its cabinet was too small to hold machinery"], 1, "A genuine machine would not lose at random, which hinted that a fallible human was playing."],
    [3, "Why did Amazon call its service 'artificial artificial intelligence'?", ["It looks like a computer doing the work, but humans quietly do it","It uses two layers of neural networks","It copies the way a chess machine thinks","It replaces human workers with software"], 0, "Like the original Turk, the service hides human effort behind something that looks automated."],
    [4, "What does the Turk's story teach about modern AI?", ["AI systems are mostly fake","AI never needs human input once built","Chess is the best test of intelligence","Much AI depends on hidden human labour, so it pays to ask who is doing the work"], 3, "Humans label data, rate answers and moderate content, so machine and human effort blur together."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"For 80 years a chess 'machine' beat all comers. Its secret still echoes in AI."});

WB.L("robot-origins", "ai", "beginner", "Where the word ‘robot’ came from",
  "The word ‘robot’ is barely a century old. It was born in a Czech play about manufactured workers who rise up against their makers.",
  [
    ["A play from Prague", "The word 'robot' comes from the Czech 'robota', meaning forced labour, and first appeared in Karel Čapek's 1920 play R.U.R.",
      "In 1920 the Czech writer Karel Čapek published R.U.R., short for Rossum's Universal Robots. It premiered in Prague in January 1921 and soon played in New York, London and beyond, carrying a new word into dozens of languages. The play is set in a factory on a remote island that mass-produces cheap, tireless, obedient artificial workers. Humans grow idle, the robots rebel, and almost all of humanity is wiped out.\n\nČapek credited his brother Josef with the word. Karel had considered 'labori', from Latin, but found it too bookish. Josef suggested 'roboti', from the Czech 'robota', meaning drudgery or forced labour, historically the unpaid work serfs owed their landlords. The name hinted at exploitation from the start."],
    ["Not metal at all", "Čapek's original robots were made of synthetic organic material, closer to androids than to metal machines.",
      "If you picture R.U.R.'s robots as clanking metal figures, you are picturing a later idea. Čapek's robots were assembled from an artificial organic material, a kind of synthetic protoplasm, mixed in vats and shaped into bodies. They looked almost human and were built in factories like any other product. In modern terms they resemble biological androids more than mechanical machines.\n\nThe metal man came mostly from later films, magazine covers and toys of the 1920s and 1930s, such as the gleaming robot in Fritz Lang's 1927 film Metropolis. Over time, 'robot' came to mean any machine that senses, decides and acts in the world."],
    ["Older dreams of automata", "People built lifelike moving machines long before anyone called them robots, but those machines could not sense or respond to the world.",
      "The dream is ancient. Greek myths described bronze guardians like Talos, said to patrol the island of Crete. In the first century AD, Hero of Alexandria wrote about devices driven by water, air and weights, including self-opening temple doors. In 1206 the engineer Ismail al-Jazari, working in what is now Turkey, described water-powered automata, including a band of mechanical musicians.\n\nIn eighteenth century Switzerland, Pierre Jaquet-Droz and his collaborators built 'The Writer', a doll-sized boy who dips a quill in ink and writes a programmable message. It still works and is displayed in a museum in Neuchâtel. These were marvels of clockwork, though none could sense or respond to the world."],
    ["Asimov's three laws", "Isaac Asimov imagined robots with built-in safety rules, and his stories show how hard it is to turn values into rules.",
      "In the 1940s the American writer Isaac Asimov grew tired of stories where robots always turned on their creators. In his 1942 story 'Runaround' he set out the Three Laws of Robotics. A robot must not harm a human or, through inaction, allow one to come to harm. It must obey human orders unless that conflicts with the first law. And it must protect itself unless that conflicts with the first two.\n\nAsimov also coined the word 'robotics'. Many of his best stories are about the laws failing in unexpected ways, an early insight into how hard it is to turn values into rules."],
    ["Real robots, old questions", "The first factory robot arrived in 1961, and Čapek's questions about labour, control and who benefits still shape debates about AI.",
      "Real robots arrived in factories, not living rooms. In 1961 the Unimate, built from George Devol's patented programmable arm and promoted by Joseph Engelberger, began work at a General Motors plant in New Jersey, lifting and stacking hot die-cast metal. It was just a heavy arm bolted to the floor, but it could be reprogrammed. Today millions of industrial robots weld, paint, pack and assemble, mostly in controlled environments.\n\nČapek's themes remain. What happens when machines do the work? Who profits? Could created beings deserve rights? Many researchers think the robot-uprising template distorts public thinking, since real AI risks are more often errors, bias, misuse and economic disruption. Technology is also about the people who build and own it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where does the word 'robot' come from?", ["A Latin word meaning 'to build'","A Czech word for drudgery or forced labour","A Greek word for 'moving statue'","An old German word for 'iron man'"], 1, "'Robota' meant forced labour, so the name carried a hint of exploitation from the start."],
    [1, "How did Čapek's original robots differ from the popular image of a robot?", ["They were made of steel plates","They were powered by clockwork","They were made of synthetic organic material, closer to androids","They were invisible machines in a factory"], 2, "The metal robot came from later films and toys; Čapek's creatures were artificial flesh."],
    [2, "What did early automata such as 'The Writer' lack that real robots have?", ["The ability to sense and respond to the world","Any moving parts at all","A shape that resembled a person","A way of being displayed in public"], 0, "Clockwork automata followed fixed mechanisms, while robots sense, decide and act."],
    [3, "What do many of Asimov's stories about the Three Laws reveal?", ["That robots can never be built safely","How hard it is to turn values into rules","That humans should avoid obeying robots","That machines will always rebel"], 1, "The laws often fail in unexpected ways, which shows how difficult it is to capture values in simple rules."],
    [4, "Why do many researchers think the robot-uprising story distorts thinking about AI?", ["Real AI risks are more often errors, bias, misuse and economic disruption","AI systems cannot make mistakes","Robots are too expensive to build","Uprisings are impossible in factories"], 0, "Realistic risks are less dramatic than rebellious machines, but they matter more to real people."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The word 'robot' is barely a century old, and it meant something grim."});

WB.L("automation-and-jobs", "ai", "beginner", "Will machines take our jobs? What history says",
  "People have feared that machines would make human work obsolete for over 200 years. The real story is more surprising, and more uncomfortable, than either side admits.",
  [
    ["The Luddites", "The Luddites objected to machines being used to cut wages, and their hardship shows new technology can leave particular people worse off for a generation.",
      "In 1811 groups of skilled English textile workers began smashing machinery at night, signing letters as the mythical 'General Ludd'. The movement lasted until 1816. The government sent thousands of soldiers, made machine-breaking a capital crime in 1812, and hanged or transported many Luddites, some to Australia. 'Luddite' is now an insult for someone who hates technology, but historians call that unfair. The Luddites objected to machines being used to cut wages, employ unskilled labour and produce shoddier goods.\n\nTheir suffering was real. Hand-loom weavers, once well paid, saw wages collapse over decades as power looms took over. Historians still debate how quickly ordinary living standards rose in the Industrial Revolution."],
    ["Where did the farmers go?", "Machines replaced most farm work, yet people moved into new kinds of jobs as cheaper food left more money to spend elsewhere.",
      "Consider farming. Around 1900, roughly two in five American workers worked on farms, and the share in Australia was also large. Today it is a small fraction in both countries, thanks to tractors, harvesters, fertilisers and better crops.\n\nA farmer in 1900 might have predicted mass unemployment. Instead, people moved into factories, offices, shops, schools and hospitals, doing jobs that had barely existed before. Rising productivity made food cheaper, which left people with more money for other things, which created demand for new kinds of work. Over the long run, this pattern has repeated again and again."],
    ["The lump of labour fallacy", "Economists warn against assuming there is a fixed amount of work, though history is no guarantee that everything will work out.",
      "A common mistake in automation debates is the lump of labour fallacy: the idea that an economy has a fixed amount of work, so if machines do more, there is less for people. History suggests otherwise. When technology makes something cheaper, people often buy more of it, and the savings get spent elsewhere. New technologies also create entirely new industries, from car repair to web design. Avoiding the fallacy doesn't guarantee a good outcome. It just means 'machines do more, so humans do less' is too simple.\n\nIn 1930 the economist John Maynard Keynes popularised the phrase 'technological unemployment', yet predicted his grandchildren might work around 15 hours a week. Neither arrived."],
    ["The ATM puzzle", "Automation usually changes the tasks within jobs before it removes jobs, and the effects can play out unexpectedly over decades.",
      "Automated teller machines spread through banks from the 1970s, and it seemed obvious that human tellers would vanish. Yet the economist James Bessen found that in the United States, teller jobs held up and even grew for decades. Cheaper branches meant banks opened more of them, and tellers shifted towards customer service and selling financial products. Teller numbers did eventually fall, mainly as online and mobile banking spread.\n\nMany economists think of a job as a bundle of tasks. Technology automates some and can make the rest more valuable. Spreadsheets automated a great deal of calculation, yet accountants and analysts grew in number as their time shifted to interpreting numbers and advising people."],
    ["Is AI different?", "AI reaches into thinking work, economists disagree about how this time will unfold, and how society handles the transition matters enormously.",
      "Past waves of automation mostly replaced muscle and routine tasks. AI can now draft text, write code, summarise documents and analyse images, reaching into work that once seemed safely human. Some economists expect the familiar pattern: disruption, then new jobs and higher living standards. Others worry the change could be faster and broader, giving workers less time to adapt, or could concentrate gains among a few companies. Honest forecasters admit deep uncertainty.\n\nWhat history does teach clearly is that how a society handles the transition matters enormously, through education, retraining, safety nets and who shares in the gains. The Luddites' real question, who benefits from new machines, has never gone away."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What were the Luddites mainly protesting?", ["Machines being used to cut wages and replace skilled work with cheaper goods","The invention of all machinery","Government taxes on textile exports","Competition from overseas craftspeople"], 0, "The Luddites weren't against machines as such; they objected to how machines were used against workers."],
    [1, "Why did cheaper food from rising farm productivity help create new jobs?", ["Farmers were forced to retrain as soldiers","People had more money to spend on other things, creating demand for new work","Food exports replaced factory production","Governments hired former farm workers"], 1, "Savings from productivity get spent elsewhere, which creates demand for new kinds of work."],
    [2, "What is the lump of labour fallacy?", ["The belief that wages never change","The idea that new technology always creates jobs","The belief that all workers are equally skilled","The mistaken idea that there is a fixed amount of work, so machines leave less for people"], 3, "The amount of work isn't fixed, because cheaper goods and new industries create new demand."],
    [3, "What did the ATM story show about automation?", ["It usually changes the tasks within jobs before it removes jobs","Machines never affect employment","Bank tellers vanished within a few years","Customers prefer talking to machines"], 0, "Tellers shifted towards customer service, and job numbers fell only later as online banking spread."],
    [4, "What does history teach clearly about automation?", ["New technology leaves every worker better off","How a society manages the transition matters enormously","Workers can safely ignore new technology","AI will repeat the past exactly"], 1, "Education, retraining, safety nets and who shares the gains shape who benefits from new machines."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The Luddites weren't against machines. Find out what they actually wanted."});

WB.L("spam-filters", "ai", "beginner", "How spam filters learned to fight junk mail",
  "Every day, filters quietly throw away enormous amounts of junk email before you ever see it. Their story is one of the first great victories of everyday machine learning.",
  [
    ["Where spam came from", "Unsolicited mass messages have been profitable since 1978 because sending a million emails costs almost nothing.",
      "On 3 May 1978, Gary Thuerk, a marketing manager at Digital Equipment Corporation, sent a message to several hundred users of ARPANET, the network that grew into the internet, inviting them to presentations of new computers. The network was meant for government and research use, and the reaction was swift and angry. Thuerk later said it led to some sales, which is why unsolicited mass email never went away. When email reached the public in the 1990s, sending a million messages cost almost nothing, so a tiny response rate could still turn a profit.\n\nThe name comes from a 1970 Monty Python sketch where Vikings chant 'Spam, Spam, Spam' until nothing else can be heard. A 1994 case, when two lawyers advertised to thousands of Usenet groups at once, brought the word wider attention."],
    ["From rules to probability", "Hand-written rules were easy to dodge, so filters learned to score mail using probability.",
      "The first defence was a list of rules: block 'make money fast', block all capitals, block known senders. Spammers adapted almost instantly by misspelling words, swapping letters for numbers, hiding text in images and changing addresses. Rigid rules also caught innocent mail, such as a doctor's email mentioning a medicine name.\n\nIn 2002 the programmer Paul Graham's essay 'A Plan for Spam' popularised a filter based on Bayesian probability. It studies emails already sorted into spam and genuine mail, and for each word works out how much more often it appears in one pile. A new email is scored by combining its most telling words: 'winner' pushes towards spam, a colleague's name the other way. It learns from your own mail."],
    ["You are the teacher", "Each time you mark a message as spam or not spam, you give the filter a labelled example to learn from.",
      "Spam filtering is a classic case of supervised machine learning: learning from examples labelled with the right answer. When you click 'report spam' or rescue a message from the junk folder, you supply those labels. Large providers combine signals from vast numbers of users, so a scam campaign reported by early recipients can be blocked for everyone else quickly.\n\nModern filters look far beyond words. They check where a message came from, how that sender has behaved before, whether links lead to known scam sites, and whether the email passes technical checks proving it came from the domain it claims, using standards called SPF, DKIM and DMARC."],
    ["The cost of mistakes", "A filter must balance catching junk against wrongly hiding real email, and cutting one kind of error usually raises the other.",
      "Every filter makes two kinds of mistakes. A false negative lets spam into your inbox, which is annoying. A false positive sends a real message to the junk folder, which can be far worse if it is a job offer, a bill or a message from a friend.\n\nFilters can be tuned to be stricter or more relaxed, but reducing one kind of error usually increases the other. Most providers lean towards letting a little spam through rather than losing genuine mail. That is why it is worth checking your junk folder occasionally, especially when you are waiting for something important, such as a booking confirmation or a password reset."],
    ["An endless arms race", "Spam and phishing keep evolving, so filters are only one layer and your own caution is the last one.",
      "Spammers and filter builders are locked in an arms race. As filters improved, many criminals moved to phishing, crafting emails that impersonate banks, delivery companies or government agencies to steal passwords and money. Some now use AI to write fluent, personalised messages without the spelling mistakes that once gave scams away.\n\nLaws help too. Australia's Spam Act 2003, enforced by the Australian Communications and Media Authority, requires commercial messages to have consent, identify the sender and include a way to unsubscribe. But no filter is perfect. The best final layer is you: slow down, check the sender, and never click a link in an unexpected message asking you to log in or pay."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did unsolicited mass email never go away?", ["Email providers refused to block it","Sending millions of messages costs almost nothing, so a tiny response rate can still profit","It was required for government networks","Most people enjoy receiving advertisements"], 1, "Near-zero cost means even a very low response rate can make spam worthwhile."],
    [1, "Why do learning filters beat hand-written rules?", ["They never make mistakes","They only block messages from known senders","They need no examples to work","They learn from sorted examples and keep up as spammers change tactics"], 3, "Rules are easily dodged, while a filter that learns from examples can adapt as the junk changes."],
    [2, "What do you do when you click 'report spam'?", ["Supply a label that helps train the filter","Delete the sender from the internet","Change the filter's rules by hand","Tell the sender to stop emailing"], 0, "Your report is a labelled example, and providers combine many users' labels to protect everyone."],
    [3, "Why do providers usually lean towards letting a little spam through?", ["Spam is harmless to everyone","Blocking spam is illegal","A wrongly hidden real message, such as a job offer or bill, can be far worse","Stricter filters cost too much electricity"], 2, "False positives can hide important mail, so providers accept some false negatives."],
    [4, "What is the best final defence against phishing?", ["Replying to the sender to confirm","Relying only on the spam filter","Clicking the link to check where it leads","Slowing down, checking the sender and not clicking links in unexpected messages"], 3, "No filter is perfect, so careful habits are the last and strongest layer of protection."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Filters hide most junk mail, but they make one costly mistake."});

WB.L("shortcut-learning", "ai", "beginner", "Clever Hans and the AI that learned the wrong lesson",
  "A horse that seemed to do maths, and computers that seemed to diagnose disease, turned out to be solving a different problem altogether. Here's how AI can be right for the wrong reasons.",
  [
    ["The horse who could count", "Clever Hans seemed to do maths but was really reading unconscious cues from the people around him.",
      "In early 1900s Berlin, the retired schoolteacher Wilhelm von Osten toured with his horse, Hans. Ask Hans to add five and three and he tapped his hoof eight times. He seemed to handle fractions, dates and even spelling, and von Osten genuinely believed his horse could think. In 1904 a commission of experts found no sign of deliberate trickery.\n\nThen the psychologist Oskar Pfungst ran careful experiments. When the questioner did not know the answer, or Hans could not see them, his accuracy collapsed. Hans was not counting. He was reading tiny, unconscious changes in posture and expression that told him when to stop tapping."],
    ["Right answers, wrong reasons", "Shortcut learning is when an AI uses an accidental pattern in its data to get right answers without learning the real thing.",
      "Clever Hans had learned something impressive, just not what people thought. Machine learning systems do the same. A model uses whatever patterns help it get right answers on its training examples, whether or not they are the ones we intended. If an accidental feature lines up with the answers, the model may latch on, because that is often easier than learning the real thing. Researchers call this shortcut learning. Unlike specification gaming, it happens even when the goal is sensible.\n\nIn 2016 researchers deliberately trained a wolf-versus-husky classifier on photos where only the wolves had snow behind them. It did well, but a highlighting tool showed it was largely looking at the snow. High accuracy alone doesn't show what a system has learned."],
    ["Real cases, one legend", "Medical AI has learned hospital and pen-mark shortcuts, while the famous tank-and-weather story is probably a myth.",
      "In a 2018 study, deep learning models trained on chest X-rays could identify which hospital an image came from, using clues such as markings and equipment. Because some hospitals had sicker patients, the hospital itself helped predict disease, and the models did worse on X-rays from elsewhere. In 2019 dermatologists reported that a skin cancer classifier was more likely to call a mole cancerous if it had been marked with a surgical skin marker, which doctors often draw on suspicious spots. The systems had partly learned doctors' habits, not the disease.\n\nA famous tale of a tank-spotting network that really detected cloudy weather is probably a myth: researchers have found no solid evidence it happened. We no longer need it, as documented examples are plentiful."],
    ["Catching shortcuts", "Testing on genuinely new data and asking what a model is looking at can reveal hidden shortcuts.",
      "Pfungst exposed Clever Hans by changing the conditions: he hid the questioner and used questions nobody in the room knew the answer to. AI researchers use similar tricks. The most important is testing a model on data from different sources than it was trained on, such as X-rays from another hospital or photos from another country. If performance drops sharply, a shortcut may be to blame.\n\nResearchers also use tools that highlight which parts of an input influenced a decision, and they deliberately alter images or text to see whether irrelevant changes flip the answer. Collecting more varied training data, where accidental patterns no longer line up with the answers, makes shortcuts harder to learn."],
    ["Thinking like Pfungst", "When an AI seems brilliant, ask what exactly it was tested on and what else could explain its success.",
      "The deepest lesson of Clever Hans is about us, not the horse. Von Osten was honest, and the experts who first examined Hans were not fools. They simply did not ask what else might explain the results.\n\nA 2021 review of dozens of published AI models for diagnosing COVID-19 from chest scans found none ready for clinical use, with many suffering from flaws such as biased or mismatched data. Impressive accuracy figures can hide a narrow trick. Whenever you hear that an AI can detect something remarkable, think like Pfungst: what exactly was it tested on, and could it be picking up on something other than what we think?"]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did Pfungst show that Hans wasn't really counting?", ["He weighed the horse before each show","He asked Hans harder arithmetic questions","He trained a second horse to compare results","Hans's accuracy collapsed when the questioner didn't know the answer or Hans couldn't see them"], 3, "Hans relied on unconscious cues from people, so removing those cues removed his accuracy."],
    [1, "What is shortcut learning?", ["A model using an accidental pattern in its data instead of the real signal","A model finishing training faster than expected","A model copying answers from another model","A model ignoring data it does not recognise"], 0, "Models take the easiest pattern that predicts the answer, even when it is not the one we intended."],
    [2, "Why did the X-ray models perform worse at other hospitals?", ["The new X-rays were lower quality","They had partly learned hospital-specific clues that did not carry over","They were trained on too many images","Doctors at other hospitals disagreed with them"], 1, "Because the hospital helped predict disease, the models leaned on clues that failed elsewhere."],
    [3, "What is a good way to expose a hidden shortcut?", ["Retest on the same photos several times","Make the model larger","Test on data from different sources than the training data","Ask the model to explain itself in words"], 2, "A sharp performance drop on genuinely new data suggests the model relied on a shortcut."],
    [4, "What should you ask when an AI seems to do something remarkable?", ["Who built it, and how large is the company","What exactly was it tested on, and could something else explain its success","How fast does it produce answers","Whether it works on a phone"], 1, "Impressive accuracy can hide a narrow trick, so check what the test really measured."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A horse that 'did maths' shows how AI can be right for the wrong reasons."});

WB.L("speech-recognition", "ai", "intermediate", "How computers learned to understand speech",
  "Talking to a phone feels effortless now, but getting a machine to hear words took seventy years of clever engineering. Here is how ‘wreck a nice beach’ became ‘recognise speech’.",
  [
    ["Why speech is hard", "Spoken words run together, vary between speakers and sound alike, so machines first turn sound into a picture called a spectrogram.",
      "Spoken words feel separate, but the sound is mostly a continuous stream with no clear gaps. Each speech sound, or phoneme, changes shape depending on its neighbours, called coarticulation. Accents, speed, mood, noise and microphone quality add more variation, and different phrases can sound almost identical: 'recognise speech' and 'wreck a nice beach'. People resolve these puzzles using knowledge of language and context. A computer starts with only a fast-changing series of air pressure measurements, sampled thousands of times a second.\n\nA key first step is slicing the audio into tiny overlapping pieces and measuring the energy at each frequency. Side by side, these form a spectrogram, a picture with time running left to right and pitch bottom to top. Vowels show as bands, and 's' as high-frequency fuzz."],
    ["Templates to statistics", "Early recognisers matched whole-word templates, but from the 1970s probability-based models beat hand-written rules.",
      "In 1952 Bell Laboratories built Audrey, which recognised spoken digits from zero to nine, mostly from a voice it had been tuned for. In 1962 IBM showed the Shoebox at the Seattle World's Fair. It understood 16 words, including digits and commands like 'plus' and 'minus', and did simple sums. These machines matched whole-word sound patterns against stored templates, which could never scale to everyday conversation.\n\nIn the 1970s the US defence research agency DARPA funded a speech understanding programme, and Carnegie Mellon's Harpy handled about a thousand words. Researchers at IBM and elsewhere then championed hidden Markov models, which calculate probabilities for phonemes and for which word follows another. With enough recorded speech, statistics beat expert rules and dominated for about three decades."],
    ["Dictation to assistants", "Dictation software arrived in the 1990s, and deep neural networks made voice assistants practical around 2010 to 2012.",
      "In 1997 Dragon Systems released Dragon NaturallySpeaking, one of the first products to let ordinary people dictate continuous speech on a home computer without pausing between words. It needed training on your voice and a quiet room, and accuracy still frustrated many users, but it was a milestone, especially for people unable to type easily.\n\nThe next leap came around 2010 to 2012, when university and technology teams showed that deep neural networks could cut error rates substantially. Combined with huge amounts of recorded speech and cloud computing, this made smartphone voice assistants and automatic video captions practical for millions of people."],
    ["Listening for the wake word", "Smart speakers listen locally for a short wake word and only then record and send audio elsewhere.",
      "A common worry is that smart speakers constantly record everything. In normal operation they work in two stages. A small, low-power model on the device itself listens continuously, but only for a short wake word, such as the assistant's name. Only when it thinks it has heard that phrase does the device start recording your request and, typically, send it to powerful computers elsewhere for full recognition.\n\nThe wake word detector is deliberately simple, so it sometimes triggers by mistake on similar sounds, accidentally capturing snippets of conversation. Most devices let you review and delete recordings and mute the microphone, which is worth knowing if privacy matters to you."],
    ["Not everyone is heard equally", "Speech recognition works better for some accents, ages and voices than others because systems learn from the recordings they are trained on.",
      "Speech systems learn from the recordings they are trained on. If those recordings mostly feature certain accents, the systems work best for people who sound like that. Studies have found commercial systems making noticeably more errors for some groups, including speakers of particular dialects, people with strong regional or non-native accents, children, older adults and people with speech impairments.\n\nThis matters more as voice becomes a way to access services, from banking to medical transcription. Developers are collecting more diverse speech data and letting systems adapt to individual voices, but equal accuracy for everyone remains an unfinished job."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is speech so hard for computers to recognise?", ["Words are recorded too slowly","Sound arrives as separate, clearly spaced words","Words run together, vary between speakers and can sound alike","Microphones can't measure air pressure"], 2, "Speech is a continuous, variable stream, and people rely on context that computers must learn."],
    [1, "Why did the statistical approach beat hand-written rules?", ["Given enough recorded speech, probabilities proved more reliable than expert rules","Statistics needed no recorded speech","Rules could only handle digits","Statistical models ran on tiny devices"], 0, "Models that calculate probabilities from lots of data handled real speech better than rules written by experts."],
    [2, "What made smartphone voice assistants practical around 2010 to 2012?", ["Smaller microphones","Deep neural networks, huge amounts of recorded speech and cloud computing","Bigger spectrogram pictures","Faster typing keyboards"], 1, "Deep networks cut error rates, and data plus cloud computing delivered them to millions of phones."],
    [3, "What does a smart speaker do before sending audio anywhere?", ["It records every conversation in the room","It asks you to press a button","It transcribes everything locally","A small on-device model listens only for a short wake word"], 3, "Full recording and recognition begin only after the device thinks it heard the wake word."],
    [4, "Why does speech recognition work worse for some speakers?", ["Some people speak too quietly","Systems learn from training recordings, so they suit the voices that dominate them","Voices of older people are illegal to record","Microphones only detect certain accents"], 1, "Less representative training data means more errors for groups who are underrepresented in it."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"How 'recognise speech' once sounded like 'wreck a nice beach' to a machine."});

WB.L("gradient-descent", "ai", "intermediate", "How AI learns: walking downhill in the fog",
  "Behind almost every modern AI lies one surprisingly simple idea: take a small step in the direction that makes your mistakes smaller, then repeat it billions of times.",
  [
    ["Walking downhill in fog", "Gradient descent trains a model by repeatedly stepping in the direction that shrinks its loss, the number measuring its mistakes.",
      "Imagine standing on a mountainside in dense fog. You can't see the valley, but you want the lowest point. So you feel the slope underfoot, step in the steepest downhill direction, and repeat. This is gradient descent, the method used to train nearly all modern neural networks. The mountain represents how wrong the model is, and each position is a different setting of its internal numbers.\n\nTo measure 'wrong', a model needs a loss function. For house prices, it compares each prediction with the real price and combines the errors into one number. For a language model, the loss measures how surprised it was by the actual next word. The model becomes very good at whatever the loss measures, which isn't always exactly what we want."],
    ["Knobs and the gradient", "The gradient tells you, for every parameter at once, which way to turn it to change the loss, so training turns each one slightly downhill.",
      "A neural network is controlled by parameters, also called weights, which you can picture as knobs. A small model might have thousands; the largest today have billions or more. Each combination of settings gives a different loss. The gradient, a tool from calculus, tells you for every knob at once which way to turn it, and how strongly, to increase the loss fastest. So you turn every knob a little the opposite way, lowering the loss slightly. The French mathematician Augustin-Louis Cauchy described the method in 1847, long before computers. What changed was the scale."],
    ["Choosing the step size", "The learning rate sets the size of each step, and a value too small or too large can ruin training.",
      "How far should you step each time? The learning rate controls it. Too small, and training crawls. Too large, and you overshoot the valley, leap up the opposite slope and may bounce around wildly or even climb higher, like taking giant strides on that foggy mountain. Engineers spend a lot of effort tuning it. A common practice is to start with larger steps and gradually shrink them, so the model can settle into a good spot."],
    ["Rough terrain, noisy steps", "Training works in practice despite rough loss landscapes, and small random batches make each step fast.",
      "Real loss landscapes are full of dips, ridges, plateaus and saddle points, which slope down in some directions and up in others. In theory you could be trapped in a local minimum, a hollow far above the true lowest point. In practice, very large networks seem to train well anyway: with millions of dimensions there is usually some direction still leading down, and many low points are roughly equally good. Why it works so reliably is still being researched.\n\nChecking every training example before each step would be painfully slow. So training uses stochastic gradient descent: each step estimates the slope from a small random batch, perhaps a few hundred examples. The path wobbles, but steps are quick, and the wobble can help avoid getting stuck."],
    ["Backpropagation's bookkeeping", "Backpropagation efficiently works out how much each weight contributed to the error, and gradient descent then takes the step.",
      "In a deep network with many layers, how do you work out how each of millions of weights contributed to the final error? Backpropagation starts with the error at the output and works backwards, layer by layer, using the chain rule from calculus to share out responsibility for the mistake.\n\nSeveral people developed versions of the idea, but a 1986 paper by David Rumelhart, Geoffrey Hinton and Ronald Williams made it widely known for training neural networks. Backpropagation calculates the slope; gradient descent takes the step. Repeated over enormous datasets, with clever variations, they are the engine of today's deep learning."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the loss function do?", ["It turns the model's errors into a single number that training tries to shrink","It sets how many examples are in each batch","It decides how many layers a network has","It stores the model's final answers"], 0, "Training needs one number measuring how wrong the model is, so it knows whether a step helped."],
    [1, "What does the gradient tell you about a model's parameters?", ["How many parameters the model needs","Which way and how strongly each one affects the loss, so it can be nudged downhill","Which training examples to delete","How long training will take"], 1, "The gradient points uphill for every parameter at once, so stepping the opposite way lowers the loss."],
    [2, "What can happen if the learning rate is too large?", ["Training becomes slow but safe","The loss function stops working","The model memorises its data perfectly","Steps overshoot the valley and training may bounce around or get worse"], 3, "Giant strides can jump straight over the low point, so step size must be tuned."],
    [3, "Why does training use small random batches?", ["Estimating the slope from a batch is far faster than checking every example","Batches make the slope exactly accurate","Random batches remove the need for a loss","Large datasets cannot be stored"], 0, "Noisy but quick steps add up to steady improvement, and the wobble can even help avoid getting stuck."],
    [4, "How do backpropagation and gradient descent work together?", ["Backpropagation picks the learning rate","Gradient descent calculates the slope and backpropagation takes the step","Backpropagation calculates the slope and gradient descent takes the step","They are two names for the same step"], 2, "Backpropagation works out each weight's share of the error, and gradient descent uses it to adjust the weights."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Modern AI learns by one simple move, repeated billions of times. Think fog."});

WB.L("word-embeddings", "ai", "intermediate", "Words as maps: how AI captures meaning",
  "Computers only understand numbers. So how can a machine know that ‘puppy’ is close to ‘dog’ and far from ‘spreadsheet’? The answer is to give every word a place on a giant map.",
  [
    ["The company words keep", "Words used in similar contexts have similar meanings, so giving words arbitrary ID numbers tells a computer nothing.",
      "In 1957 the linguist J. R. Firth wrote: 'You shall know a word by the company it keeps.' You can learn a lot about a word from its neighbours. Meet 'wombok' in 'the wombok was crisp and fresh' and 'she chopped the wombok for the stir-fry', and you'd guess it's a vegetable. 'Coffee' and 'tea' both appear near 'cup', 'hot' and 'morning'.\n\nComputers need numbers, but the simplest scheme, numbering each word, fails. 'Aardvark' is 1, 'abacus' is 2, and the numbers carry no meaning: to such a system 'cat' and 'kitten' are as unrelated as 'cat' and 'carburettor'. Better to give related words related numbers, so what a model learns about 'dog' transfers to 'puppy'."],
    ["A map of meaning", "An embedding gives each word a list of numbers that places it on a learned map, where related words sit close together.",
      "The solution is an embedding: a list of numbers, often a few hundred long, acting as coordinates that place each word on an enormous map. An ordinary map needs two numbers; an embedding uses hundreds of dimensions we can't picture, but the mathematics works the same way. 'Sydney', 'Melbourne' and 'Brisbane' cluster in one region, 'happy', 'glad' and 'cheerful' in another. People don't choose the positions. The model learns them from huge amounts of text.\n\nIn 2013 a Google team led by Tomas Mikolov released word2vec. Directions on the map seemed to carry meaning: 'king' minus 'man' plus 'woman' landed nearest 'queen'. The famous examples were somewhat selected, and the arithmetic is less neat in general, but meaning had been captured in geometry."],
    ["Learning our biases too", "Embeddings absorb the stereotypes in the text they learn from, which matters when they are used in real decisions.",
      "Because embeddings learn from human writing, they also learn human prejudices. In 2016 researchers showed that word2vec embeddings trained on news articles completed analogies such as 'man is to computer programmer as woman is to homemaker'. In 2017 another team found that embeddings reproduced many associations measured in psychological tests of human bias, linking some names, genders and groups with pleasant or unpleasant words.\n\nThe embeddings weren't programmed to be biased; they faithfully mapped the patterns in their text. This matters because embeddings sit inside search engines, recruitment tools and recommendation systems, where such associations can quietly shape real decisions. Researchers have developed methods to measure and reduce these effects."],
    ["Same word, new meaning", "Modern models create contextual embeddings, recalculating a word's position for each sentence it appears in.",
      "The original embeddings gave each word one fixed position. But 'bank' means something different in 'river bank' and 'bank loan', and 'bat' could be an animal or cricket gear. One point has to blur these together.\n\nFrom around 2018, new models, soon dominated by the transformer design, began producing contextual embeddings, where a word's position is recalculated for each sentence using every surrounding word. Today's chatbots work this way. Text is broken into pieces called tokens, each starting with an embedding, and many layers then refine them, mixing in context until the model has a rich representation of the whole passage."],
    ["Maps of everything", "Embeddings now represent sentences, images, songs and products, powering search by meaning and recommendations.",
      "The embedding idea spread far beyond single words. Whole sentences and documents can be embedded, so a search engine can find pages matching the meaning of your question even if they share no exact words with it. Songs, products and films can be embedded based on who enjoys them, helping recommendation systems suggest similar items.\n\nSome systems embed images and text into the same shared space, so a photo of a beach lands near the words 'sand and waves', letting you search your photo library by description. Whenever an app finds things that are 'similar' in a way that feels like understanding, there is a good chance it is measuring distances on a learned map."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is numbering words 1, 2, 3 a poor way to give them to a computer?", ["The numbers become too large to store","Computers cannot read numbers in order","The numbers carry no information about meaning or similarity","Numbers cannot represent rare words"], 2, "With arbitrary IDs, 'cat' and 'kitten' look as unrelated as 'cat' and 'carburettor'."],
    [1, "What does an embedding do?", ["Translates a word into another language","Counts how often a word appears in a book","Replaces each word with its dictionary definition","Places each word as a point on a learned map, with related words close together"], 3, "Positions are learned from text, so words used in similar contexts end up near each other."],
    [2, "Why do embeddings reproduce stereotypes?", ["Engineers deliberately add them","They learn from human writing, which contains those patterns","They only use old books","They cannot tell names from other words"], 1, "Embeddings faithfully map the patterns in their text, including human prejudices."],
    [3, "How do contextual embeddings differ from the original ones?", ["A word's position is recalculated for each sentence using the surrounding words","They give every word the same position","They work only for single words","They remove tokens from the text"], 0, "This lets the model separate meanings such as a river bank and a bank loan."],
    [4, "How can embeddings help a search engine?", ["By storing every page word for word","By ranking pages purely by length","By finding pages that match the meaning of your question even without shared words","By blocking unusual searches"], 2, "Because meaning becomes distance on a map, similar ideas can be found even when the wording differs."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"To an AI, 'puppy' sits near 'dog' on a map. Here's how words get a place."});

WB.L("expert-systems", "ai", "intermediate", "Expert systems: when AI ran on rules",
  "In the 1980s, the future of AI was supposed to be computers stuffed with the knowledge of human experts. Businesses spent fortunes on it. Then the boom went bust.",
  [
    ["Bottling expertise", "Expert systems captured a specialist's knowledge as if-then rules, and their power came from specialised knowledge rather than general reasoning.",
      "In the 1960s and 1970s, researchers such as Edward Feigenbaum at Stanford argued for dropping general problem-solvers and focusing on deep knowledge of one narrow field. Interview human experts, write what they know as hundreds or thousands of rules, such as 'if the patient has a fever and a stiff neck, consider meningitis', and let an inference engine chain the rules together. These programs became known as expert systems.\n\nAn early success was DENDRAL, begun at Stanford in 1965 with Feigenbaum, geneticist Joshua Lederberg and chemist Carl Djerassi. It helped chemists work back from the fragments of mass spectrometry to a molecule's structure, ranking possible structures. Some results appeared in scientific papers. Its lesson: power came less from clever general reasoning than from specialised knowledge."],
    ["MYCIN explains itself", "MYCIN matched specialists at advising on blood infections and could show its reasoning, yet it was never used on real patients.",
      "In the 1970s Edward Shortliffe and colleagues at Stanford built MYCIN, which identified bacteria causing serious blood infections and recommended antibiotics. Several hundred rules carried 'certainty factors', since medical evidence is rarely clear-cut. In a formal evaluation, experts rated its advice as good as or better than that of infectious disease specialists. Yet it was never used in hospitals, because of slow question sessions, the computers of the day and unresolved questions of responsibility if it was wrong.\n\nIts strength was transparency. Asked why it wanted to know something, MYCIN showed the rule it was applying; asked how it reached a conclusion, it listed the chain of rules. Modern neural networks are far harder to interpret, so explainable AI researchers try to recover some of that clarity for medicine, lending and law."],
    ["The boom", "In the 1980s expert systems left the laboratory, and one called XCON saved its company tens of millions of dollars a year.",
      "By the early 1980s, expert systems had left the laboratory. A famous example was XCON, developed with Carnegie Mellon University for Digital Equipment Corporation. It configured complex computer orders, working out which cables, cabinets and components a customer's system needed, a job that had produced costly errors. XCON grew to thousands of rules, and the company estimated it saved tens of millions of dollars a year.\n\nSuch successes sparked a boom. Companies set up AI departments, start-ups sold specialised hardware and software, and in 1982 Japan launched its ambitious Fifth Generation Computer project, prompting rival government programmes in Britain, Europe and the United States."],
    ["The knowledge bottleneck", "Getting expert knowledge into rules was slow and fragile, and the resulting systems failed badly outside their narrow domain.",
      "The trouble was getting knowledge in. Experts often cannot fully explain what they do, because much of their skill is intuition built over years. Interviewing them and turning their answers into rules was painstaking, a problem Feigenbaum called the knowledge acquisition bottleneck. Systems also became hard to maintain, since a new rule could clash with existing ones in unexpected ways.\n\nAnd they were brittle. Step slightly outside the narrow domain, or give them a situation their rules did not anticipate, and they could fail badly, with no common sense to fall back on. A medical rule system knew nothing about the world beyond its rules, not even that patients are people."],
    ["Bust and legacy", "The expert systems market crashed in the late 1980s, but rule-based software lives on and machine learning rose partly in response.",
      "By the late 1980s, expectations had run far ahead of results. The market for specialised AI computers collapsed around 1987, as ordinary desktop machines became powerful enough to do the job more cheaply. Funding was cut, companies folded, and the field entered what became known as the second AI winter.\n\nYet expert systems never disappeared. Rule-based software still checks insurance claims, flags suspicious bank transactions, calculates tax and guides call centre staff, though rarely under the AI label. Machine learning rose partly as an answer to the knowledge bottleneck: rather than asking experts to write down rules, let computers learn patterns directly from data."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where did a system like DENDRAL get its power?", ["From clever general reasoning about any problem","From a very fast computer","From specialised knowledge packed into it","From learning on millions of examples"], 2, "Expert systems showed that deep knowledge of one narrow field could beat general reasoning."],
    [1, "What was a key strength of expert systems like MYCIN?", ["They could show the chain of rules behind a conclusion","They could learn new rules by themselves","They worked well outside their field","They never needed a doctor's input"], 0, "Explicit rules let users check the reasoning step by step and spot faulty logic."],
    [2, "What did XCON do for Digital Equipment Corporation?", ["It designed new computer chips","It trained sales staff","It configured complex computer orders, saving the company tens of millions of dollars a year","It diagnosed faults in customers' machines"], 2, "Real business success like XCON helped spark the 1980s expert systems boom."],
    [3, "Why were expert systems called brittle?", ["Their hardware broke easily","Outside their narrow domain they could fail badly, with no common sense to fall back on","They ran too slowly to be useful","Experts refused to use them"], 1, "Rules covered only what had been anticipated, so unexpected situations caused failures."],
    [4, "How did the expert systems story help shape machine learning?", ["Machine learning copied their rule lists","Experts proved that learning from data was impossible","Rule-based software was banned in banks","Machine learning rose partly as an answer to the knowledge bottleneck, learning patterns from data"], 3, "Rather than asking experts to write rules, researchers let computers learn patterns directly from data."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In the 1980s, firms spent fortunes bottling human expertise. Then the boom went bust."});

WB.L("bitter-lesson", "ai", "advanced", "The bitter lesson: why scale keeps winning",
  "For seventy years, researchers have tried to make AI smarter by teaching it what humans know. Again and again, they were beaten by simpler methods with more computing power. One famous essay asked what that means.",
  [
    ["The bitter lesson", "Sutton argued that general methods which scale with computation eventually beat methods built on human knowledge.",
      "In March 2019, Rich Sutton, a pioneer of reinforcement learning, published a short essay called ‘The Bitter Lesson’. His claim: looking back over decades of AI research, general methods that make the most of computation, chiefly search and learning, win by a large margin in the end.\n\nResearchers kept building in their own understanding, such as how chess masters think or how language works. It helped in the short term and felt satisfying. But it plateaued, and was overtaken by methods that simply scaled up. That is why the lesson is ‘bitter’: it suggests much of that careful effort is wasted. Sutton later shared the Turing Award, computing’s highest honour, with Andrew Barto."],
    ["Computation compounds", "Methods that improve with more computation keep gaining almost automatically, and their gains follow predictable curves.",
      "The engine behind the lesson is the long fall in the cost of computation. For decades the number of transistors on a chip roughly doubled every couple of years, a trend known as Moore’s law, and specialised AI chips have added more. A method that improves with more computation gets better almost automatically. One built on hand-crafted human knowledge improves only as fast as people can write that knowledge down.\n\nScaling can even be forecast. In 2020, OpenAI researchers reported that a language model’s error falls along a smooth power-law curve as size, data and computation grow. In 2022, DeepMind’s Chinchilla work showed many large models had too little data for their size."],
    ["Chess and Go", "In chess and Go, raw search and self-play eventually beat approaches built on human expertise.",
      "Chess was the first clear example. Many researchers hoped to win by capturing how grandmasters understand the board. Instead, IBM’s Deep Blue beat Garry Kasparov in 1997 largely through enormous search, examining huge numbers of positions per second, combined with expert-tuned evaluation.\n\nGo repeated the story with a twist. In 2016, DeepMind’s AlphaGo beat Lee Sedol, having learned partly from human games and partly from playing itself. The following year, AlphaGo Zero learned purely from self-play, given nothing but the rules, and became stronger still. Human knowledge had been a helpful crutch that was eventually not needed."],
    ["Speech and vision", "In speech and vision, systems that learned from data and computation overtook ones built on expert-designed knowledge.",
      "The same pattern played out elsewhere. Early speech recognition tried to apply linguistic knowledge about how words and sounds work. From the 1970s, statistical methods that learned probabilities from recorded speech pulled ahead, and deep neural networks took over in the 2010s.\n\nIn computer vision, researchers spent years hand-designing features, carefully engineered methods for detecting edges, corners and textures. Then in 2012, a deep network that learned its own features from over a million labelled images beat them decisively. Each time, the experts’ knowledge was genuine and hard-won. It just mattered less than a general learning method with enough data and computation."],
    ["The critics", "Critics say scale alone has limits, and whether it will keep delivering is still hotly debated.",
      "The bitter lesson has thoughtful critics. Successful methods do contain human insight: convolutional networks for vision build in the idea that a pattern means the same thing wherever it appears in an image, and that idea came from people.\n\nThere are practical limits too. High-quality data is finite, and training ever larger models consumes vast amounts of money, electricity and chips. Some researchers argue that current systems still struggle with reliable reasoning, learning from few examples and understanding the physical world, and that new ideas, not just more scale, will be needed. Even so, the past decade’s biggest leaps came less from elegant theories of intelligence than from learning methods scaled to a size earlier generations could barely imagine."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the main claim of Sutton’s ‘Bitter Lesson’ essay?", ["General methods that scale with computation tend to win in the long run","Expert knowledge should always be built into AI systems first","AI progress depends mainly on cleverly designed algorithms","Search methods are better than learning methods in every field"], 0, "Over decades, methods that make the most of growing computation overtook those built on human understanding."],
    [1, "Why does a method that scales with computation have a long-term advantage?", ["It improves almost automatically as computing gets cheaper and faster","It needs far less data than hand-crafted systems","It copies human reasoning more accurately over time","It avoids any need for large amounts of electricity"], 0, "Falling computing costs compound over decades, while hand-written knowledge grows only as fast as people can write it down."],
    [2, "What did AlphaGo Zero show about human knowledge in Go?", ["It learned purely from self-play and became stronger than earlier versions","It needed a larger library of human games to improve","It could only win when experts tuned its evaluation","It played best when copying famous human strategies"], 0, "Without any human games, self-play alone produced a stronger player, so human knowledge was not essential."],
    [3, "What happened in computer vision around 2012?", ["A deep network that learned its own features beat hand-designed ones","Engineers perfected hand-designed edge and corner detectors","Speech methods were adapted to recognise images","Researchers abandoned large labelled image sets"], 0, "Learning features from a million labelled images outperformed years of expert feature engineering."],
    [4, "Which is a criticism of relying on scale alone?", ["High-quality data is finite and huge models cost vast resources","Larger models always get worse at reasoning tasks","Human insight plays no part in successful methods","Computing power has stopped getting cheaper entirely"], 0, "Data limits, costs and gaps in reasoning are why some researchers think new ideas are needed too."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"For seventy years, clever AI lost to brute force. What does that tell us?"});

